# Reviewer 5 — Algorithms/complexity perspective

Same-assistant specialist perspective; not external peer review.

**Recommendation: require a costed access/state model.** Q15 is a valid linear-kernel argument on the full real domain. It is neither a Boolean bit bound nor a lower bound on arbitrary encodings. For fixed b-bit states, the basic bound is log2 of the number of distinct attainable answer vectors. Efficient encoding, decoding and updating do not follow from counting states.

Q16 should say that all protocols with a cumulative t-coordinate budget have the same indistinguishability relation as Q_t. It should not say one transcript reveals all queries. Resetting the horizon after destructive updates is refuted by parity. The new harness implements explicit episodes and residual coordinates, so its operational contract can be inspected rather than inferred from timing labels.

O(r2^r) is arithmetic work after profiles exist; bit precision and input reads remain separate. Polynomial in listed factor dimensions may be exponential in n. Low r and small kappa therefore do not constitute a practical amortization theorem.

I agree with the Fourier reviewer on the main gap and with the KC reviewer that a sharply scoped negative theorem could be sufficient progress. Acceptance requires explicit preprocessing/state/update/query bounds and a falsifiable regime, without extending a restricted lower bound to all data structures. **SEND RESULTS/QUESTION TO THEORY.**
